const angles = [
  ['Start with the person, not the product','Document how the primary user enters the room, approaches the shower, transfers or remains seated, reaches controls, manages a towel, and exits when wet. A future user may have different strength, balance, vision, or caregiver needs. Those observations turn a broad product search into a functional brief.'],
  ['Measure the complete route','Record door widths, door swings, hallway turns, bathroom floor area, toilet and vanity projections, baseboards, finished-wall dimensions, ceiling height, drain location, supply-wall location, and subfloor type. Photograph a tape measure in each image so the scale remains clear later.'],
  ['Separate shell size from usable space','A model name often describes nominal dimensions. Finished interior space can be smaller, while flanges and framing requirements can make the rough opening different. Obtain the current specification sheet and installation instructions for the exact model and drain orientation before demolition.'],
  ['Treat water management as a system','A low entry changes how water is contained. Floor slope, drain capacity, waterproofing transitions, spray direction, curtain or glass placement, room ventilation, and the drying routine must work together. A powerful showerhead aimed at an open entry can defeat an otherwise careful design.'],
  ['Coordinate structure before finishes','Grab bars, folding seats, doors, wall panels, and some controls need solid backing or manufacturer-approved anchors. Plan reinforcement while walls are open. Mark its dimensions and photograph it before the finished surface hides the framing.'],
  ['Choose controls by reach and use','A valve that looks centered may be unreachable from a seat or outside the spray. Consider whether the user must start the water before entering, adjust temperature while seated, or hand a spray wand to a caregiver. Hose length, docking height, and control force matter.'],
  ['Build an itemized budget','Ask the quote to separate the shower system, freight, demolition, disposal, carpentry, subfloor repair, plumbing, electrical work, waterproofing, wall finish, accessories, permits, and cleanup. Allow a contingency for concealed damage but require approval before added work.'],
  ['Verify responsibilities in writing','Clarify who measures, orders, receives, inspects for freight damage, pulls permits, protects the home, shuts off water, handles debris, performs each trade, and completes warranty registration. Product warranty and labor warranty are separate promises.'],
  ['Plan for maintenance','Ask how the drain is accessed, which cleaners are allowed, how seams are inspected, whether caulk is part of routine maintenance, how a folding seat is tightened, and where replacement parts come from. Easy maintenance protects both appearance and performance.'],
  ['Use standards carefully','The ADA Standards govern covered facilities and define specific configurations. A private home is not automatically required to follow the ADA, and a single product cannot make an entire room compliant. Even so, the standards can be a valuable reference when a qualified designer applies them to the correct setting.']
];

